# Git push and GitHub CLI auth

## Symptom

Bare `git push` to an `https://github.com/...` remote often fails in agent/sandbox sessions:

```text
fatal: could not read Username for 'https://github.com': No such device or address
```

`gh` may still work if the user has run `gh auth login` on the machine.

## Before push or PR

1. `gh auth status` — if not logged in, ask the user to run `gh auth login` (see [.cursor/docs/github-workflow.md](../docs/github-workflow.md)).
2. Prefer **`gh`** for issues and PRs: `gh issue view`, `gh pr create`, `gh pr checks`.

## Push when `git push` fails

Use the token from `gh` (do not print or commit the token):

```bash
GH_TOKEN=$(gh auth token)
git push "https://x-access-token:${GH_TOKEN}@github.com/sea2709/jade-court.git" HEAD:your-branch-name
```

Replace `your-branch-name` with the branch being published. For first push, same URL with `-u` is not required if tracking already exists locally.

Then create the PR:

```bash
gh pr create --head your-branch-name --base main --title "..." --body "..."
```

If the branch is **already** on `origin`, `gh pr create` alone is enough.

## Permissions

Request `required_permissions: ["all"]` (or at least network + git) for `git push` and `gh` when the sandbox blocks them.

## Local fix (human)

- `gh auth setup-git` — configure Git to use `gh` as credential helper for HTTPS.
- Or use SSH: `git@github.com:sea2709/jade-court.git` instead of HTTPS.
